EC2: "I rent and manage a server."

Lambda: "AWS manages the servers; I only provide my function."

That's the main difference.

===========

Yes — this is the confusing part. Let me make it very simple.

Imagine a restaurant 🍽️

When you use AWS Lambda, AWS still has physical computers (servers) somewhere in its data centers.

You do not own or manage those computers.

Think of it like this:

                 AWS DATA CENTER
        ┌───────────────────────────┐
        │   Physical AWS Servers    │
        │   Physical AWS Servers    │
        │   Physical AWS Servers    │
        │   Physical AWS Servers    │
        └─────────────┬─────────────┘
                      │
                  AWS manages
                      │
                      ▼
                AWS Lambda
                      │
                Your Python code

You only see:

AWS Lambda
    ↓
Your Python function

AWS takes care of the underlying servers.

========

Compare with EC2

With EC2, you are renting a virtual server:

AWS physical hardware
        ↓
      EC2
        ↓
Your operating system
        ↓
Your Python / FastAPI

You have to manage things such as the server configuration, OS, updates, and application environment.

======

With Lambda
AWS physical hardware
        ↓
AWS manages everything
        ↓
Lambda
        ↓
Your Python function

You don't manage the underlying server.

====

So why is it called "serverless"?

Because "serverless" doesn't mean servers don't exist.

It means:

You don't have to manage or provision the servers yourself.

AWS says Lambda is a serverless compute service and charges based on usage.

One sentence to remember

EC2: "I rent and manage a server."

Lambda: "AWS manages the servers; I only provide my function."

That's the main difference.

=====
